<h2 id="h-mathews-lift-29-5-ibo-speed">Mathews Lift 29.5 IBO Speed: What 348 fps Really Means</h2>
<p>The Mathews Lift 29.5 IBO speed is rated up to 348 fps on most listings. Lancaster Archery notes that figure at the 80% let-off setting, and one retailer lists 343 fps. That small gap matters less than you think, but it is worth understanding why it exists.</p>
<p>IBO speed is a standardized lab test: a long draw length, a high draw weight and a light arrow. Very few hunters shoot that exact combination. Your real arrow speed depends on:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Your draw length.</strong> A shorter draw stores less energy, so expect less speed than the rating.</li>
  <li><strong>Your draw weight.</strong> Hunting at the low end of your limb class drops speed.</li>
  <li><strong>Arrow weight.</strong> A heavy hunting arrow is slower but carries more momentum.</li>
  <li><strong>Let-off.</strong> Higher let-off settings usually give up a little speed, which is one reason figures differ between listings.</li>
  <li><strong>String accessories.</strong> A peep, D-loop and silencers all add weight to the string.</li>
</ul>
<p>So instead of treating 348 as your number, plug your own draw length, draw weight and arrow weight into a calculator. That gives you a realistic estimate for sight tape and broadhead choice.</p>
