<h2 id="h-design-intent-and-mechanical-behavior">Design Intent: Why the Lift 29.5 Exists</h2>
<?= figure('Mathews-Lift-29.5-2.webp', 'Mathews Lift 29.5 compound bow riser and cams', 'The Lift 29.5 packs a full hunting setup into a 29.5-inch frame.') ?>
<p>The Lift 29.5 was not designed to top speed charts. It was designed to feel calm. Mathews built the Lift line around a smooth draw, a solid wall and a shot that settles quickly, then offered it in two lengths so hunters could choose between maneuverability and stability.</p>
<p>Every bow stores energy at full draw and dumps it into the arrow at release. Whatever does not go into the arrow turns into vibration and noise. A bow that controls that leftover energy feels dead in the hand, holds still through follow-through and is quieter in the woods. That is the Lift 29.5&rsquo;s whole personality.</p>

<h2 id="h-compact-axle-to-axle-without-instability">Compact Axle-to-Axle Without the Usual Instability</h2>
<p>At 29.5 inches, this bow sits firmly in compact territory. Short bows are easy to swing in a ground blind or around a tree trunk, but they can feel twitchy: steeper string angles, less resistance to torque and more sensitivity to small grip errors.</p>
<p>The Lift 29.5 offsets that with a moderate 6-inch brace height and a mass weight just under 4 pounds. That combination gives the bow enough heft to hold steady without feeling like a club at the end of a long sit. For most hunters who shoot inside 40 yards, the extra handling ease outweighs the small stability edge of a longer bow.</p>
<?= callout('tip', 'Long draw? Think about the 33', 'Shooters near the top of the 30-inch draw range sometimes find sharper string angles on compact bows pinch the nose or make the peep harder to align. If that is you, try the Lift 33 before deciding.') ?>

<h2 id="h-draw-cycle-and-full-draw-control">Draw Cycle and Full-Draw Control</h2>
<p>The Lift draw cycle is built to ramp up evenly instead of stacking hard and then dumping into the valley. That matters when you are drawing slowly on a deer that is already alert. A predictable draw lets you keep your bow arm still and your eyes on the animal.</p>
<p>At full draw, you choose between 80% and 85% let-off. Here is how that choice plays out:</p>
<?= data_table(
    ['Let-off', 'Holding feel', 'Best for'],
    [
        ['80%', 'Slightly heavier hold, firmer feel against the wall', 'Shooters who want a touch more speed and a more active hold'],
        ['85%', 'Lighter hold, easier to wait out a slow-moving animal', 'Long holds in a stand, cold weather, smaller-framed shooters'],
    ],
    'Choosing let-off on the Mathews Lift 29.5'
) ?>
<p>Neither is wrong. If your typical hunt involves long waits at full draw, the higher let-off is easier on your shoulders. If you mostly shoot targets and short-hold shots, 80% feels crisper.</p>

<h2 id="h-shot-reaction-and-vibration-control">Shot Reaction and Vibration Control</h2>
<p>This is where the Lift 29.5 aims to stand out. The Lift line is positioned around a quiet shot that settles quickly, with little buzz in the grip. For a hunter, that is more than comfort: less noise means less chance an animal reacts to the sound before the arrow gets there, and less movement means you can watch the hit.</p>
<p>You can help it along with the right accessories. A short hunting stabilizer adds forward weight that damps leftover motion, and string silencers trim noise further. A <?= amz('hunting bow stabilizer 6 inch', 'compact hunting stabilizer') ?> is one of the cheapest upgrades you can make to any short bow.</p>

<h2 id="h-balance-and-grip-interaction">Balance and Grip Interaction</h2>
<p>Balance decides what the bow does after the shot. A bow that tips back or rotates sideways adds motion that shows up as wider groups. The Lift 29.5 is generally described as neutral in the hand, and you can tune that with your sight and quiver choice.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Relax your hand.</strong> Let the grip sit on the pad of your thumb, not across your palm. A tight grip adds torque.</li>
  <li><strong>Watch the quiver.</strong> A loaded quiver on the bow changes balance. Practice with it on if you hunt with it on.</li>
  <li><strong>Add a sling.</strong> A <?= amz('compound bow wrist sling', 'wrist sling') ?> lets you keep your hand open without worrying about dropping the bow.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="h-efficiency-instead-of-raw-speed">Efficiency Instead of Raw Speed</h2>
<p>A rated speed up to 348 fps puts the Lift 29.5 in a strong spot for a compact hunting bow, but the real value is how it spends its energy. A bow that wastes less energy as vibration feels smoother with heavier arrows, which is what most hunters shoot anyway.</p>
<p>If you are building a hunting setup, choose an arrow that matches your draw weight and draw length, then let the calculator tell you how much energy you will deliver. For deer and larger game, many hunters prefer a heavier arrow over a flatter trajectory. Good starting points are quality <?= amz('carbon hunting arrows 300 spine', 'carbon hunting arrows') ?> paired with a fixed or mechanical broadhead that flies well from your bow.</p>

<h2 id="h-hunting-focused-performance">Hunting-Focused Performance</h2>
<p>The Mathews Lift 29.5 bow makes the most sense in tight places:</p>
<ul class="checks">
  <li>Treestands where limbs and branches crowd your swing</li>
  <li>Ground blinds where a long bow can hit the roof or window frame</li>
  <li>Spot-and-stalk hunts where you carry the bow for miles</li>
  <li>Thick timber where shots are short and fast</li>
</ul>
<p>A sub-4-pound mass weight helps on long walks, and a quiet shot helps at close range. If most of your shots are inside 40 yards from a stand or blind, this platform fits your hunting style well.</p>
